  "id": "Compound::DB00641 - Gene::1555",
  "verbalization": "Simvastatin is implicated in the regulation of Cyp2b6 through a series of interconnected pathways. The evidence suggests that Simvastatin may act as a regulator of Cyp2b6 via a common mechanism involving the Hmg-Coa Reductase Inhibitor and Statin class of compounds. This is supported by the fact that Simvastatin causes multiple syndromes, including Facial Paresis, Ophthalmoplegia, and Hypersensitive Syndrome, all of which are associated with Fluvastatin or other statins. These syndromes are linked to Cyp2b6 through the binding of these compounds, indicating a potential role in the modulation of Cyp2b6 activity. The presence of a common ancestor in the pathway—Hmg-Coa Reductase Inhibitor—suggests a shared regulatory mechanism, possibly involving the inhibition of Cyp2b6 or its downstream effects. The integration of these pathways highlights a potential role for Simvastatin in modulating Cyp2b6 through a broader network of interactions, emphasizing the importance of considering the broader pharmacological context in understanding its effects.",
